\section{Open Science}
\label{app:openscience}
All artifacts are publicly available on GitHub~\cite{trivedi2026randomnessartifact}, at
\url{https://github.com/devharsh/sok-randomness-181-artifact}. Release v1.0 matches this paper.
The repository contains the incident dataset (\texttt{incidents.csv}, \texttt{incidents.json}),
the remedy codings and the counterfactual coding of every incident with a one-line reason
(\texttt{remedies.csv}, \texttt{counterfactuals.csv}), the dataset schema, the scripts that
generate the coverage matrix and check the tables of this paper against the dataset
(\texttt{build\_coverage.py}, \texttt{build\_dataset.py}), the notebook behind
Appendix~\ref{app:math}, and the editable draw.io sources of Figures~\ref{fig:pipeline}
and~\ref{fig:coldcard}. No proprietary data or special hardware is required, and every primary
source is cited with a resolvable identifier.

\section{Ethical Considerations}
\label{app:ethics}
This work is a systematization of already-public information. Every incident we discuss has been
disclosed by its vendor, published at a peer-reviewed venue, or both, and our reconstruction of the
2026 \coldcard{} case relies exclusively on the vendor's own technical postmortem and a public
independent engineering analysis~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder,block2026coldcard,trm2026coldcard}.
We introduce no new vulnerability, no exploit code, and no attack against any system that is not
already remediated and publicly documented. The mechanisms we describe, including the offline
enumeration of a reduced seed space, are presented only at the level of detail already available in
the cited primary sources, and each is paired with its deployed remedy so that the material is more
useful to defenders than to attackers. Because all cases are disclosed, coordinated
vulnerability disclosure is not applicable. The study involves no human subjects and no personal or
private data. On-chain figures are aggregate and were reported publicly by forensic analysts. We
judge the benefit to defenders and standards bodies to outweigh the marginal risk of collecting
public facts in one place.

\section{Effective Entropy and Brute-Force Cost}
\label{app:math}
A seed carrying $H$ bits of min-entropy ranges over at most $2^{H}$ values, and no deterministic
post-processing can enlarge this set, so hashing leaves the count of possible seeds
unchanged~\cite{rfc4086,aumasson2024serious}. An adversary who can reproduce the seed
inputs enumerates the $2^{H}$ candidates and, for each, derives the BIP-39 seed, the BIP-32 master
key, and one address, then tests membership against a set of funded addresses~\cite{bip32,bip39}.
Expected work is about $2^{H-1}$ derivations, against a design target of $H=128$. For
\coldcard{}, the published analyses give different values of $H$ (Section~\ref{sec:coldcard}).
On the Mk2 and Mk3, the vendor estimates about $40$ bits and Block an upper bound of about
$40.7$~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder,block2026coldcard}. On the Mk4, Mk5, and Q, the vendor
estimates about $72$ bits, crediting the secure-element material mixed into the
state~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder}, whereas Block counts at most \bits{32} distinguishable
output streams once the fallback state and call history are fixed, within a raw ceiling of about
\bits{73.3}~\cite{block2026coldcard}. We therefore give the cost at $H=32$, $H=40.7$, and
$H=72$. At $H=72$ the arithmetic below gives $2^{71}/10^{8}$ seconds, about $7.5\times10^{5}$
years, which is infeasible unless the attacker can narrow the device state.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/bruteforce.pdf}
\caption{Expected brute-force time as a function of effective seed min-entropy, at two
derivation rates. Spaces of \bits{32} and \bits{40.7}, the smaller published estimates for
\coldcard{}, are exhausted in minutes to days, whereas 72 bits, the vendor's estimate for later
devices, and the 128-bit design target are infeasible. Recomputed in the accompanying notebook.}
\Description{A log-scale plot of expected brute-force time in seconds against effective entropy
in bits, with two curves for one million and one hundred million derivations per second, marking
the 32-bit and 40.7-bit reduced spaces as feasible and the 128-bit target as infeasible.}
\label{fig:bruteforce}
\end{figure}

Writing $r$ for the sustained derivation rate, the expected wall-clock time is $t \approx
2^{H-1}/r$. At a conservative single-accelerator rate $r=10^{6}$ derivations per second, an
$H=32$ space is exhausted in about $2^{31}/10^{6}\approx 2.1\times10^{3}$ seconds, under an hour.
At a cluster rate $r=10^{8}$ it falls to tens of seconds. An $H=40.7$ space takes about
$2^{39.7}/10^{6}\approx 9\times10^{5}$ seconds on one accelerator, roughly ten days, and a few
hours at $r=10^{8}$. By contrast $H=128$ requires on the order of $2^{127}$ derivations,
which is infeasible by any margin (Figure~\ref{fig:bruteforce}). Because the attacker tests each candidate against the whole
population of funded addresses at once, using a hash-set or Bloom filter for constant-time
membership, the cost is amortized across all victims rather than paid per wallet, which explains
how thousands of addresses were swept in a single campaign. The accompanying notebook measures
this scaling on synthetic identifiers: enumeration throughput falls by less than a factor of two
as the target population grows from a single identifier to one million, confirming that the work
is governed by the size of the candidate space and not by the number of victims.

Two user-layer defenses can be quantified in the same terms, which is useful because they are the
only ones that survive a total generator failure. Physical dice contribute
$\log_2 6 \approx 2.5850$ bits per roll, and the vendor states that 50 or more private rolls
protect a seed from this defect on their own~\cite{coinkite2026advisory}. The vendor's documentation asks
for $50$ rolls for 128-bit security and $99$ rolls for 256-bit
security~\cite{coldcard2026dice}. The first clears its target, at $129.2$ bits, and the second
does not, at $255.9$ bits, because $\lceil 256/\log_2 6\rceil=100$. The shortfall of
$0.089$ bits is operationally irrelevant, and we record it only because it is the same
off-by-a-fraction entropy bookkeeping this paper systematizes, appearing here in the
mitigation advice rather than in the generator. A BIP-39 passphrase acts as a salt in the PBKDF2 derivation of
the seed, so for independent contributions the effective min-entropy is additive: a mnemonic
carrying only $40$ bits combined with a passphrase carrying $80$ bits yields roughly $120$ bits,
which restores infeasibility even though the mnemonic itself was generated by a broken
generator~\cite{bip39}. This is the arithmetic behind the vendor's guidance that seeds made with
enough private dice rolls, or protected by a strong passphrase, are at lower
risk~\cite{coinkite2026advisory}.

The consumption-stage (\stage{6}) failures rest on a different and exact identity. If one ECDSA
private key $d$ signs two messages with hashes $h_1,h_2$ using the same nonce $k$, the two
signatures share their first component $r$, and from $s_1=k^{-1}(h_1+r d)$ and $s_2=k^{-1}(h_2+r d)$
modulo the group order $n$ one recovers
\[
k=\frac{h_1-h_2}{s_1-s_2}\bmod n,\qquad
d=\frac{s_1 k-h_1}{r}\bmod n .
\]
No search is required: a single repeated nonce, visible on a public ledger, yields the key
directly, which is why the PlayStation~3, Android, and cryptocurrency nonce failures were
immediately
catastrophic~\cite{fail0verflow2010ps3,michaelis2013randomly,bos2014ecc,breitner2019biased}. Even
without a full repeat, a few bits of nonce bias per signature suffice for lattice-based key
recovery, the basis of the Minerva and TPM-Fail
attacks~\cite{breitner2019biased,jancar2020minerva,moghimi2020tpmfail}. The full derivations and
numeric checks are provided in the accompanying notebook.

\section{Per-stage incident narratives}\label{app:narratives}
The following narratives expand the rows of Table~\ref{tab:incidents} stage by stage. The
\coldcard{} fault path and the 2026 wallet cluster are treated in Section~\ref{sec:incidents}.

\subsection{Source and seeding failures (\stage{1}, \stage{4})}
The oldest and most numerous class starves the generator of entropy. Netscape derived SSL
session keys from the process identifier, its parent, and the time of day, all guessable, so
keys could be predicted in seconds~\cite{goldberg1996netscape}. The pattern returned at Internet
scale in 2012: headless routers, firewalls, and servers that generated keys immediately after
boot shared entropy pools, and researchers factored or reconstructed the private keys of
more than a hundred thousand TLS and SSH hosts, and found RSA moduli sharing a single prime across
unrelated devices~\cite{heninger2012mining,lenstra2012ron}. ROCA is a subtler seeding failure:
Infineon's on-card key generation drew primes from a structured subset, cutting effective
entropy far enough that the modulus alone revealed the key by Coppersmith's
method~\cite{nemec2017roca}. What unites this class is that every value looks fine in isolation.
The deficit is only visible across a population or against the mathematics of key generation.

\subsection{Algorithm and back-door failures (\stage{3})}
Here the generator is qualitatively wrong. MIFARE Crypto-1 drove its challenges with a 16-bit
linear-feedback register that an attacker could roll forward, collapsing the card's
security~\cite{garcia2008mifare,nohl2008rfid}. PHP applications minted password-reset tokens and
session identifiers from non-cryptographic generators seeded from the request
clock~\cite{argyros2012php}. Two cryptocurrency tools repeated the pattern with unusual clarity.
The Libbitcoin Explorer \texttt{bx seed} command drew wallet seeds from the Mersenne Twister
seeded with a 32-bit system clock value, reducing the entire global key space to roughly four
billion candidates. The resulting Milk Sad vulnerability (CVE-2023-39910) was exploited in a
coordinated theft costing hundreds of victims millions of dollars across several
chains~\cite{milksad2023}. The Profanity vanity-address generator likewise derived Ethereum keys
from a 32-bit seed, which allowed attackers to reconstruct the generator state and sweep the
addresses it had produced~\cite{1inch2022profanity}. In both cases the generator was
qualitatively wrong for the purpose and the seed was far too small, and in both cases the outputs
looked perfectly ordinary on the chain until someone thought to enumerate them.
The \dualec{} generator is the defining subversion case: its
elliptic-curve constants admit a secret relationship that lets whoever chose them predict all
future output from a small sample, a back door flagged in 2007, standardized regardless, shown
practically exploitable in TLS in 2014, and traced through the Juniper incident in
2016~\cite{shumow2007dualec,bernstein2016dualec,checkoway2014dualec,checkoway2016juniper}. The
Juniper case also touches \stage{$\ast$}, because an attacker-substituted curve point turned a
dormant back door into a live one. Even standardized, well-analyzed algorithms can fail here:
CTR\_DRBG loses its state to a cache side channel when implementations skip
reseeding~\cite{cohney2020blackswan}.

\subsection{State-lifecycle failures (\stage{5})}
Generators that are correct at instantiation can still repeat or expose state. Resetting a
virtual machine to a snapshot rewinds its generator, so a server reissues the same nonces and
session keys after every reset, an especially dangerous property once cloud deployment made
snapshots routine~\cite{ristenpart2010vmreset,everspaugh2014whirlwind}. DUHK showed that a
FIPS-certified ANSI X9.31 generator with a seed key hardcoded in firmware allowed passive state
recovery and decryption of VPN traffic~\cite{cohney2018duhk}. Fork-safety gaps in library
generators fall here as well~\cite{strenzke2016openssl}, as do the forward-security and
state-recovery weaknesses identified in early analyses of the Linux
generator~\cite{gutterman2006linux}.

\subsection{Consumption failures (\stage{6})}
Even a flawless generator is undone by a consumer that reuses or leaks a nonce. The
PlayStation~3 signed with a constant ECDSA nonce, so two signatures exposed Sony's master
code-signing key~\cite{fail0verflow2010ps3}. The same algebra recurs wherever nonces repeat or
are biased: repeated nonces are visible on the Bitcoin ledger and have been mined to steal
funds, and even a few biased bits per nonce enable lattice key
recovery~\cite{bos2014ecc,breitner2019biased}. The Android \texttt{SecureRandom} defect drove
this from the generator side, producing colliding nonces that emptied
wallets~\cite{michaelis2013randomly}. Minerva and TPM-Fail show the leak need not be a full
repeat: timing that reveals only the bit-length of each nonce is enough to recover keys from
smart cards and trusted platform modules by lattice methods~\cite{jancar2020minerva,moghimi2020tpmfail}.


\subsection{Randomness as a covert channel}
\label{sec:covert}
Every failure considered so far degrades randomness. A distinct and in some ways more troubling
class does the opposite: it keeps the output statistically perfect while choosing it
adversarially, so that the random field becomes a channel for smuggling secrets out of a device.
Young and Yung formalized this as kleptography, showing how a black-box implementation can embed
a secretly embedded trapdoor whose outputs are indistinguishable from honest ones to everyone
except the attacker holding the corresponding private key~\cite{young1997kleptography}. The
signature nonce is the natural carrier, because it is secret, per-signature, and published
implicitly through the signature itself.

The modern demonstration is Dark Skippy, disclosed to roughly fifteen vendors in March 2024. A
malicious signing device derives its nonces from the seed rather than from randomness, placing
the first half of a compromised seed in the nonce of one signature and the second half in
another. The attacker then runs Pollard's kangaroo algorithm against the public nonce points to
solve for the secret nonces and reassemble the seed. Two signatures suffice, and the attack is
not specific to any product but is a general capability that any malicious signer
possesses~\cite{darkskippy2024}. Nothing about the resulting signatures is anomalous: they verify
correctly, the nonces are uniformly distributed, and no statistical test on the device's output
can distinguish them from honest ones.

This class matters to our systematization for a structural reason. Every remedy in
Section~\ref{sec:remedies} that improves entropy \emph{quality} is powerless here, because the
entropy is already perfect. The problem is that the device, not the user, chooses it. Defenses
must therefore constrain \emph{who} contributes the randomness rather than how good it is, which
is precisely what the anti-exfil protocols of Section~\ref{sec:remedies} do. It also sharpens the
thesis of this paper. We argued that statistical testing cannot detect predictable-but-uniform
output. Kleptography shows it equally cannot detect adversarially-chosen-but-uniform output. Both
evade the same defense for the same reason, namely that uniformity is a property of the output
distribution while security depends on how the value was chosen.

\section{Remedies by stage, in detail}\label{app:remedystage}
This appendix expands the remedy families of Table~\ref{tab:remedies} in pipeline order, asking
what defends each stage and noting where one remedy reaches across several stages.

\textbf{Source and seeding (\stage{1}, \stage{2}, \stage{4}).} The modern baseline is a vetted
physical source with continuous health tests, as codified in NIST SP
800-90B~\cite{nist80090b}, feeding a generator that blocks until it has accumulated enough
entropy. The deployment lesson of the 2012 boot-entropy failures is precisely this last point:
Linux's \texttt{getrandom} system call and the move away from non-blocking early reads exist to
prevent key generation before the pool is seeded~\cite{heninger2012mining,dodis2013devrandom}.
Where a device cannot gather entropy locally, an entropy-quality protocol can have a party prove
its key was generated with good randomness~\cite{corrigangibbs2013ensuring}.

\textbf{Algorithm and state (\stage{3}, \stage{5}).} Using a standardized CSPRNG such as
HMAC-DRBG or a Fortuna-style design closes the qualitative-weakness gap at low
friction~\cite{nist80090a,kelsey1999yarrow}, and hardware generators can themselves be given a
provable-security treatment rather than being trusted on assertion~\cite{shrimpton2015intel},
provided implementations reseed and protect state.
The CTR\_DRBG cache attacks are a reminder that the algorithm choice must be paired with state
hygiene~\cite{cohney2020blackswan}. Fork-safety and virtual-machine reseeding hooks close the
lifecycle gaps behind the VM-reset and Whirlwind
findings~\cite{ristenpart2010vmreset,everspaugh2014whirlwind}.

\textbf{Consumption (\stage{6}).} The single highest-value remedy for signatures is to remove the
dependence on per-signature randomness. RFC 6979 derives the ECDSA nonce deterministically from
the message and private key, and EdDSA builds this in by
construction~\cite{rfc6979,bernstein2012ed25519}. Had deterministic nonces been universal, the
PlayStation~3, Android, and cryptocurrency nonce failures could not have
occurred~\cite{fail0verflow2010ps3,michaelis2013randomly,breitner2019biased}. Full determinism,
however, trades one risk for another, and the mechanism is worth stating precisely because it
explains the direction the standards have taken. Since the nonce is now a function of the key and
message alone, an adversary who induces a fault in one of two signatures over the same message
obtains two signatures that share a nonce, whereupon the private key falls out by exactly the
algebra of nonce reuse given in Appendix~\ref{app:math}. Deterministic ECDSA and EdDSA are
demonstrably vulnerable to this~\cite{poddebniak2018fault}. Repeating an identical computation
also lowers the noise floor for side-channel analysis, a concern acute on smart cards and
embedded devices. The current direction is therefore \emph{hedged} signing, which folds a
fresh secret value into the deterministic derivation. The property that makes this the right
default is graceful degradation: the added randomness need not be perfect, and even if the same
value is reused across two messages the scheme is no weaker than deterministic ECDSA, so a failed
generator cannot reintroduce the catastrophic algebraic key recovery of fully randomized
signing~\cite{cfrg2025hedged}. Neither variant helps key \emph{generation}, which is where
\coldcard{} failed.

\textbf{Anti-exfiltration (\stage{6}).} Against the covert-channel attacks of
Section~\ref{sec:covert}, entropy quality is beside the point and the remedy must instead deny
the signer sole control of the nonce. Sign-to-contract, deployed as anti-klepto or anti-exfil,
achieves this: the device first commits to a nonce point, the host then supplies its own random
contribution, and the device must produce a nonce that verifiably incorporates that contribution
by the agreed tweak. The host checks the final nonce against the earlier commitment, so a device
that tried to steer the nonce toward an encoding of the seed would have had to predict the host's
contribution in advance~\cite{bitbox2019antiklepto,blockstream2020antiexfil}. This closes at the
signing stage the same gap that commit-reveal entropy checks close at key generation, and the two
together are the only remedies in our catalog that constrain a device the user cannot inspect.

\textbf{Defense in depth (\stage{4}--\stage{6}).} Hedged cryptography hashes the secret key, the
message, and whatever randomness is available together, so that output stays secure as long as
\emph{any} input is unpredictable~\cite{bellare2009hedged}. This is the most general structural
defense against bad randomness, and its absence from mainstream libraries is a gap we revisit in
Section~\ref{sec:discussion}. User-supplied entropy, such as \coldcard{}'s optional dice rolls
and BIP-39 passphrase, is a coarse but real instance of the same idea, and the vendor's guidance
treats enough private dice rolls as protection against the generator defect~\cite{coinkite2026advisory,bip39}.

\textbf{Physical and component defenses (\stage{1}, \stage{2}).} Against the induced failures of
Section~\ref{sec:induced}, code-level measures are useless and hardware measures are the only
answer. The state of practice in secure elements combines a generator evaluated under a
recognized scheme such as AIS-31 or PTG.2 with environmental sensors, active shielding, and
glitch and voltage detection, so that a device pushed outside its operating envelope halts rather
than emitting degraded output~\cite{ledger2026coldcard}. The design principle worth extracting is
\emph{fail-closed}: a generator that stalls on a health-test failure is safe, whereas one that
returns a value anyway converts a detected fault into a silent compromise, which is exactly the
failure mode of the Zen 5 \texttt{RDSEED} defect~\cite{amd2025rdseed}. Where a single component
cannot be trusted at all, the response is architectural. Mavroudis \etal{} show that a device can
be assembled from several independently sourced cryptographic chips such that the output remains
secure unless a threshold of them is subverted, which converts a supply-chain compromise from a
total break into a tolerated fault~\cite{mavroudis2017touch}. Combining independent generators,
for example by mixing an on-chip source with a secure-element source, is the same idea in
miniature, though it only helps if the combining step actually consumes both inputs, a caveat the
\coldcard{} reseed illustrates painfully~\cite{block2026coldcard}.

\textbf{Verifiable entropy (\stage{$\ast$}).} The most direct answer to a device that silently
substitutes a weak generator is to make the device \emph{prove} it used the entropy it claims.
Trezor's entropy check does this with a commit-reveal protocol: the device commits to its own
entropy contribution before the host sends its share, the host then requests extended public keys
as a fingerprint of the resulting candidate wallet, and on rejection the device reveals its
contribution so the host can check it against the commitment and rebuild the candidate
itself~\cite{trezor2025entropycheck}. A device that ignores the host's contribution, or that draws
its own from a predictable source, cannot pass repeated rounds. This is the only remedy in our
catalog that attacks the \stage{$\ast$} surface from outside the device, and it generalizes: any
setting with a second party present at key generation can demand a proof of freshness rather than
trusting an assertion, an idea with a research pedigree in entropy-quality
protocols~\cite{corrigangibbs2013ensuring}.



